---
import { withBase } from '../../data/site';
import { UI, counterpartPath, type Lang } from '../../data/i18n';
import Icon from '../icons/Icon.astro';

const { lang } = Astro.props;
const ui = UI[lang];
const base = lang === 'en' ? '/en' : '';
const langToggleHref = withBase(counterpartPath(Astro.url.pathname, lang));
---

<section class="hero">
  <h1 class="hi">{ui.homeGreeting}</h1>
</section>

{
  lang === 'zh' ? (
    <section class="prose-home">
      <p>
        这几年一直在 AI 创业公司做产品。工作后才发现，那些本来不值得人持续投入注意力、却不得不处理的事情，反而持续占用人的时间。于是我正在做
        Origin
        这款产品，替人安排运动和饮食，追踪日常花销，和处理工作上的任务。
      </p>
      <p>
        打磨 Origin
        时，原本只想把精力放在产品本身，却不断需要反复与编程 agent
        沟通、排错。这件事让我产生了做第二款产品 Craft
        的冲动，让人把注意力花在做什么、为什么做、做出来的是不是自己想要的结果上。
      </p>
      <p>
        过去几次创业在没有正反馈的情况下，公司会安排人处理更多任务。这种现象也让我意识到，当一个组织无法降低方向上的不确定性时，很容易转而增加执行上的确定性。这些经历让我长期关注一个问题：什么样的组织环境能让人愿意投入、敢于判断、能够创造？
      </p>
      <p>
        我非常喜欢阅读，他人的文字让我敢于离开不喜欢的工作环境，更主动寻找真正想做的事情。也让我意识到钱可以让我对不喜欢的事情说&ldquo;不&rdquo;。生活中总有让我困惑和好奇的问题，于是我通过写作把模糊的东西想清楚，也许我的视角也能给正在阅读的你多一个参考。
      </p>
    </section>
  ) : (
    <section class="prose-home">
      <p>
        These past few years I've been building products at AI startups. Once I started working,
        I noticed something: the things that don't deserve anyone's attention — yet still have to
        be dealt with — are exactly the ones that keep taking up our time. So I'm building Origin,
        a product that arranges your exercise and meals, tracks your daily spending, and handles
        your work tasks.
      </p>
      <p>
        While polishing Origin, I meant to focus on the product itself, but kept getting pulled
        into back-and-forths with coding agents, debugging. That experience gave me the urge to
        build a second product, Craft — one that lets people spend their attention on what to do,
        why to do it, and whether what gets built is actually what they want.
      </p>
      <p>
        In my past startups, when positive feedback was missing, the company would respond by
        assigning people more tasks. It made me realize: when an organization can't reduce
        uncertainty about direction, it tends to increase certainty about execution instead.
        These experiences left me with a question I keep coming back to: what kind of environment
        makes people willing to commit, brave enough to judge, and able to create?
      </p>
      <p>
        I love reading. Other people's words gave me the courage to leave work environments I
        didn't like and go look for what I truly wanted to do. They also made me see that money
        lets me say &ldquo;no&rdquo; to what I dislike. Life is full of questions that puzzle and
        intrigue me, so I write to think the vague ones through — and maybe my perspective can
        offer you, the reader, one more reference.
      </p>
    </section>
  )
}

<section class="home-section">
  <h2>{ui.homeDir}</h2>
  <ul class="link-row">
    <li>
      <a href={withBase(`${base}/work`)} data-sec="work">
        <Icon name="work" />{ui.homeWork}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={withBase(`${base}/projects/`)} data-sec="projects">
        <Icon name="projects" />{ui.homeProjects}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={withBase(`${base}/blog/`)} data-sec="blog">
        <Icon name="blog" />{ui.homeBlog}
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={langToggleHref} data-sec="lang" hreflang={lang === 'zh' ? 'en' : 'zh-CN'} aria-label="Switch language">
        <Icon name="lang" />{ui.langToggle}
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <h2>{ui.homeFind}</h2>
  <ul class="link-row">
    <li>
      <a href="https://github.com/jichuncai" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        <Icon name="github" />GitHub
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="mailto:joe@example.com">
        <Icon name="mail" />Email
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>
</section>
